.SH NAME
ktm-guide-type-aliases \- Type aliases (chapter 21 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBtype Name = T;\fR gives a type a second name. The alias is the type itself, not a new one: the two are interchangeable everywhere. This chapter covers declaring aliases, naming long types with them, what "the same type" means, aliases of arrays and generic instances, and aliases in modules.
.SH Declaring an alias
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
type Celsius = f64;
type Count = i64;

i32 main() {
    Celsius t = 21.5;
    Count n = 3;
    printf("%.1f %d\en", t, n);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
21.5 3
.EE
.RE
.PP
The name can stand for any type: a slice, a pointer, a function pointer, a struct, an array, a generic instance.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
type Text = u8[];
type Name = u8 *;
type Handler = fn(i64) bool;

bool positive(i64 n) {
    return n > 0;
}

i32 main() {
    Text t = "hello";
    Name n = "ada";
    Handler h = &positive;
    printf("%d %s %d\en", t.len, n, h(5));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
5 ada 1
.EE
.RE
.SH Naming long types
.PP
Aliases make signatures read as what they mean, especially with function pointer types:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
type Text = u8[];
type Handler = fn(i64, Text) bool;
type Celsius = f64;

struct Reading {
    Text place;
    Celsius temp;
};

type Readings = Reading[];

bool show(i64 i, Text place) {
    printf("%d: %.*s\en", i, place.len, place.ptr);
    return true;
}

Celsius warmest(Readings all, Handler each) {
    Celsius best = \-273.15;
    for (i64 i = 0; i < all.len; i++) {
        each(i, all[i].place);
        if (all[i].temp > best) {
            best = all[i].temp;
        }
    }
    return best;
}

i32 main() {
    Reading r[3] = {{"Iasi", 18.5}, {"Cluj", 21.0}, {"Sibiu", 16.25}};
    printf("warmest %.1f\en", warmest(r, &show));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0: Iasi
1: Cluj
2: Sibiu
warmest 21.0
.EE
.RE
.SH An alias is the same type
.PP
A \fBCelsius\fR and an \fBf64\fR pass to each other with no conversion; \fBsizeof\fR, \fBtypeof\fR and error messages see the real type:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
type Celsius = f64;
type Text = u8[];

i32 main() {
    Celsius c = 21.5;
    f64 f = c;
    Text t = "hello";
    u8[] s = t;
    printf("%.1f %.*s %d %s\en", f, s.len, s.ptr, sizeof(Text), typeof(c));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
21.5 hello 16 f64
.EE
.RE
.PP
An alias of a struct is that struct: values of either name assign to each other, and the struct's methods work through the alias.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x, y; };

i64 Point.sum() {
    return self.x + self.y;
}

type Pos = Point;

i32 main() {
    Point a = {1, 2};
    Pos b = a;
    printf("%d %d\en", b.y, b.sum());
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2 3
.EE
.RE
.PP
A method can be defined through the alias too: \fBi64 Pos.sum()\fR is the method \fBPoint.sum\fR\&.
.PP
Because an alias is not a new type, it doesn't stop units from being mixed up:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
type Celsius = f64;
type Fahrenheit = f64;

void heat(Celsius c) {
    printf("heating to %.1f C\en", c);
}

i32 main() {
    Fahrenheit f = 100.0;
    heat(f);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
heating to 100.0 C
.EE
.RE
.PP
For a type that the compiler keeps apart, wrap the value in a struct: two structs never convert into each other (see Structs and unions (see ktm-guide-structs-and-unions(7))).
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
struct Celsius { f64 v; };
struct Fahrenheit { f64 v; };

void heat(Celsius c) {
}

i32 main() {
    Fahrenheit f = {100.0};
    heat(f);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
expected 'Celsius' got 'Fahrenheit'
.EE
.RE
.SH Fixed arrays and generic instances
.PP
An alias of an array type includes the size, and declares arrays of it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
type Buf = u8[8];
type Matrix = f64[2][2];

i32 main() {
    Buf b;
    b[0] = 'k';
    Matrix m = {{1.0, 2.0}, {3.0, 4.0}};
    printf("%d %c %d %.1f\en", sizeof(Buf), b[0], sizeof(Matrix), m[1][0]);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
8 k 32 3.0
.EE
.RE
.PP
An alias names one instance of a generic type, with its arguments written out:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import vec, map;

type Ints = vec.Vec<i64>;
type Ages = map.Map<u8 *, i64>;

i32 main() {
    scoped Ints nums = vec.new<i64>();
    nums.push(3);
    nums.push(4);
    scoped Ages ages = map.new<u8 *, i64>();
    ages.set("ann", 41);
    printf("%d %d %s\en", nums.len, ages.getOr("ann", 0), typeof(nums));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2 41 vec.Vec<i64>
.EE
.RE
.PP
An alias can't take type parameters of its own: \fBtype ByName<V> = map.Map<u8 *, V>;\fR is not ktm. Name each instance instead.
.SH Where aliases go
.PP
Aliases are declared at the top level of a file. In a program an alias can only name types declared above it, and is only usable below it:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    Later x = 1;
    return 0;
}

type Later = i64;
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
Type `Later` has not been declared
.EE
.RE
.PP
Two aliases that name each other are an error.
.PP
In a module, any order works, and an alias is private unless it is \fBpub\fR; outside the module it is \fBmodule.Name\fR (see Modules and packages (see ktm-guide-modules-and-packages(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
module units;

pub type Meters = f64;      // units.Meters for the module's importers
type Scratch = u8[64];      // only this module sees it
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBtype\fR is a keyword only at the start of such a declaration: a variable or a field can still be called \fBtype\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
type Id = i64;

struct Token {
    u8 *type;
    Id id;
};

i32 main() {
    Token t = {"word", 7};
    printf("%s %d\en", t.type, t.id);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
word 7
.EE
.RE
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtype Name = T;\fR makes \fBName\fR another name for \fBT\fR; both are the same type everywhere.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBT\fR can be any type: numbers, pointers, slices, fixed arrays, function pointers, structs, generic instances with their arguments.
.IP \(bu 2
Aliases have no type parameters of their own.
.IP \(bu 2
They are declared at the top level; in a program before their use, in a module in any order.
.IP \(bu 2
In a module an alias is private unless \fBpub\fR, and is \fBmodule.Name\fR outside.
.IP \(bu 2
For a distinct type the compiler keeps apart, use a struct.
.SH SEE ALSO
